WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.179 --> 00:00:06.140
We’ve all been there, you’ve just won
the lottery, and you’re kicking back in

00:00:06.140 --> 00:00:08.980
the hot tub, indulging in your favourite beverage.

00:00:08.980 --> 00:00:12.040
Then, all of a sudden it’s gone.

00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:16.740
Your alarm clock rudely awakens you and you
come to the sudden realisation that your life

00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:20.080
is considerably less glamorous than in your
dreams.

00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:21.620
Oh bollocks.

00:00:21.620 --> 00:00:27.170
But then something strange happens, five minutes
passes by and you’ve forgotten most of,

00:00:27.170 --> 00:00:32.119
if not all of your dream and that’s if you’re
lucky, sometimes we have dreams we’re not

00:00:32.119 --> 00:00:38.140
even aware of, because we can’t remember
even having them, after we’ve awoken.

00:00:38.140 --> 00:00:39.260
Why is this?

00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:41.810
Why do we so easily forget our dreams?

00:00:41.810 --> 00:00:43.070
Let’s find out.

00:00:43.070 --> 00:00:50.940
The brain is the last major mystery of the
human body.

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:55.809
There are so many aspects of the brain that
continuously baffle scientists and one of

00:00:55.809 --> 00:00:57.790
those is sleep.

00:00:57.790 --> 00:01:01.870
Scientists aren’t even one hundred per cent
sure why we even need sleep.

00:01:01.870 --> 00:01:06.610
So when it comes to understanding dreams,
we’re still very much in the dark.

00:01:06.610 --> 00:01:08.600
We have many theories of course.

00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:12.850
One of the most popular theories is that when
we sleep our brains are filtering through

00:01:12.850 --> 00:01:18.380
the day’s memories and deciding which ones
are important enough to keep permanently and

00:01:18.380 --> 00:01:20.320
which to ditch.

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:24.000
Scientists think dreams are a visual representation
of this process.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:28.479
Based on that theory it makes sense that in
order to understand dreams further we should

00:01:28.479 --> 00:01:33.770
study the two parts of the brain that are
larger responsible for processing memories.

00:01:33.770 --> 00:01:36.869
Those are the hippocampus and the neocortex.

00:01:36.869 --> 00:01:42.579
And that’s exactly what researchers at the
California Institute of Technology did.

00:01:42.579 --> 00:01:47.470
First let’s talk about the hippocampus,
which you may be surprised to know, is not

00:01:47.470 --> 00:01:53.610
a college campus for hippos it’s actually
named after its resemblance to a seahorse.

00:01:53.610 --> 00:01:56.390
This is the part of our brains where memories
are formed.

00:01:56.390 --> 00:02:01.189
However, it’s thought that during sleep
these memories get transferred to the outer

00:02:01.189 --> 00:02:06.500
layer of our brain, the neocortex, where they
are permanently stored.

00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:07.960
Think of it like a digital camera.

00:02:07.960 --> 00:02:13.239
The hippocampus is the camera’s sensor;
the neocortex is the memory card.

00:02:13.239 --> 00:02:18.329
For a memory to be transferred from the hippocampus
to the neocortex the two parts of the brain

00:02:18.329 --> 00:02:23.489
have to be connected via neurons, which are
responsible for transmitting information around

00:02:23.489 --> 00:02:24.819
our brains.

00:02:24.819 --> 00:02:28.370
If your brain were the Internet then your
neurons would be the routers.

00:02:28.370 --> 00:02:34.059
The hippocampus and the neocortex do this
by firing electrical signals from their neurons

00:02:34.059 --> 00:02:38.200
at exactly the same time, several times per
second.

00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:42.790
Imagine the two parts of your brain are having
a conversation about the events of the day

00:02:42.790 --> 00:02:44.609
i.e. your memories.

00:02:44.609 --> 00:02:49.430
When they both fire their neurons at the same
time and they are in sync we know they are

00:02:49.430 --> 00:02:52.040
communicating with each other.

00:02:52.040 --> 00:02:53.849
Scientists call this “neural chatter”.

00:02:53.849 --> 00:02:58.939
Researchers at Caltech used brand new technology
to listen in to these two parts of the brain

00:02:58.939 --> 00:03:01.599
in rats, whilst they were sleeping.

00:03:01.599 --> 00:03:05.859
They found that whilst the rats were in a
stage of the sleep cycle known as slow-wave

00:03:05.859 --> 00:03:12.279
sleep, more commonly referred to as “deep
sleep”, the hippocampus and neocortex where

00:03:12.279 --> 00:03:14.290
perfectly in sync.

00:03:14.290 --> 00:03:16.980
Their neurons were firing at the same time.

00:03:16.980 --> 00:03:21.389
From this we can presume that the two parts
of the brain were talking to each other and

00:03:21.389 --> 00:03:22.879
memories were being recorded.

00:03:22.879 --> 00:03:25.359
But here’s where dreams come in.

00:03:25.359 --> 00:03:30.699
During the slow-wave sleep phase we don’t
have any dreams, this is known as a dreamless

00:03:30.699 --> 00:03:32.049
sleep state.

00:03:32.049 --> 00:03:37.400
The researchers then did the same thing whilst
the rats were in the rapid eye movement or

00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:38.400
R.E.M.

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:39.579
sleep stage.

00:03:39.579 --> 00:03:45.729
What they observed, once and for all, answered
why we have a hard time remembering our dreams.

00:03:45.729 --> 00:03:46.729
During the R.E.M.

00:03:46.729 --> 00:03:51.959
sleep stage the hippocampus and neocortex
were still firing their neurons, but not at

00:03:51.959 --> 00:03:53.489
the same time.

00:03:53.489 --> 00:03:54.909
They were no longer in-sync.

00:03:54.909 --> 00:04:01.559
To put it in other words, they were still
having conversations, but not with each other.

00:04:01.559 --> 00:04:02.879
This means during R.E.M.

00:04:02.879 --> 00:04:08.440
sleep memories don’t get recorded to our
brain’s databank, the neocortex and R.E.M.

00:04:08.440 --> 00:04:12.650
sleep is when the majority of our dreams occur.

00:04:12.650 --> 00:04:18.120
So the memories of our dreams are still being
created, they’re just not being stored permanently.

00:04:18.120 --> 00:04:23.750
We remember only very faint aspects of our
dreams, because very occasionally the neurons

00:04:23.750 --> 00:04:29.990
might, by chance, fire at the same time, but
the majority of the time they don’t.

00:04:29.990 --> 00:04:34.199
Some people believe this is because dreams
are non-essential to life; they’re no more

00:04:34.199 --> 00:04:37.930
than a by-product of the creation of new memories
during our sleep.

00:04:37.930 --> 00:04:40.330
So what’s the point in remembering them?

00:04:40.330 --> 00:04:42.110
They don’t serve a purpose.

00:04:42.110 --> 00:04:48.240
But for whatever reason, we don’t remember
our dreams, simply because during a certain

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:53.770
stage of our sleep, two very important parts
of our brain, which are both responsible for

00:04:53.770 --> 00:04:57.180
memory, don’t get on with each other very
well.

00:04:57.180 --> 00:05:01.360
To be honest, it’s probably for the best;
some of the weird-ass stuff we do in our dreams

00:05:01.360 --> 00:05:16.899
is definitely best forgotten.

